{"name":"Phenomenal Peach","hex":"#ffcba2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"phenomenal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Very remarkable; highly extraordinary; amazing.","Perceptible by the senses through immediate experience."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/phenomenal"},{"term":"peach","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any tree of species Prunus persica, native to China and now widely cultivated throughout temperate regions, having pink flowers and edible fruit.","Soft juicy stone fruit of the peach tree, having yellow flesh, downy, red-tinted yellow skin, and a deeply sculptured pit or stone containing a single seed."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to the color peach.","Particularly pleasing or agreeable."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peach"}],"entries":[{"title":"Phenomenon","blocks":[{"text":"A phenomenon, sometimes spelled phaenomenon, is an observable event. The term came into its modern philosophical usage through Immanuel Kant, who contrasted it with the noumenon, which cannot be directly observed. Kant was heavily influenced by Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz in this part of his philosophy, in which phenomenon and noumenon serve as interrelated technical terms. Far predating this, the ancient Greek Pyrrhonist philosopher Sextus Empiricus also used phenomenon and noumenon as interrelated technical terms."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Streichholz.jpg/330px-Streichholz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phenomenon"},{"title":"Peach","blocks":[{"text":"The peach is a deciduous tree that bears edible juicy fruits with various characteristics. Most are simply called peaches, while the glossy-skinned, non-fuzzy varieties are called nectarines. Though from the same species, they are regarded commercially as different fruits."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Illustration_Prunus_persica_clean_no_descr.jpg/330px-Illustration_Prunus_persica_clean_no_descr.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach"},{"title":"Peach (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Peach is a color that is named for the pale color of the interior flesh of the peach fruit. Like the color apricot, the color peach is paler than most actual peach fruits and seems to have been formulated primarily to create a pastel palette of colors for interior design."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Space_Fire.jpg/500px-Space_Fire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Space_Fire.jpg/1280px-Space_Fire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Space_Fire.jpg/500px-Space_Fire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A comparison between a candle flame on Earth (left) and in a microgravity environment, such as that found on the International Space Station (right).\nSee also: Electrical phenomena and Optical phenomena\nThe same burning phenomenon is observed, but different flame shape and colouring phenomena are also observed.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpace_Fire.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Cloud_chamber_bionerd.jpg/500px-Cloud_chamber_bionerd.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Cloud_chamber_bionerd.jpg/1280px-Cloud_chamber_bionerd.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Cloud_chamber_bionerd.jpg/500px-Cloud_chamber_bionerd.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cloud chamber phenomena. Scientists use phenomena to refine some hypotheses and sometimes to disprove a theory. See also animated version.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACloud_chamber_bionerd.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Autumn_Red_peaches.jpg/500px-Autumn_Red_peaches.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Autumn_Red_peaches.jpg/1280px-Autumn_Red_peaches.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Autumn_Red_peaches.jpg/500px-Autumn_Red_peaches.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAutumn_Red_peaches.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Peachblossoms3800ppx.JPG/250px-Peachblossoms3800ppx.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Peachblossoms3800ppx.JPG/330px-Peachblossoms3800ppx.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Peachblossoms3800ppx.JPG/250px-Peachblossoms3800ppx.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APeachblossoms3800ppx.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Prunus_persica%EF%BC%88%E8%8A%B1%E6%A1%83%EF%BC%894035837.JPG/250px-Prunus_persica%EF%BC%88%E8%8A%B1%E6%A1%83%EF%BC%894035837.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Prunus_persica%